## 2. Header Analysis
SPF: pass | DKIM: pass | DMARC: pass — all pass because the message was sent through a genuine Gmail account, not a spoofed domain. Authentication passing here confirms Gmail's mail servers, not sender trustworthiness — a key distinction from samples 01-03.

## 3. Attachment Analysis
Contains a PDF attachment (not a body link) impersonating a Brazilian tax authority (IRPF/Receita Federal) balance-release notice. The PDF's content is effectively a full-page image with an invisible link annotation covering the entire page:
`/URI(https://okokoaksoa.z13.web.core.windows.net/)`
— clicking anywhere on the "document" redirects to this URL.

## 4. URL / Infrastructure Analysis
Destination hosted on Microsoft Azure Static Website hosting (*.z13.web.core.windows.net) — abuse of a trusted cloud platform to evade domain-reputation blocklisting. As of analysis, DNS resolution for the subdomain fails entirely (storage account removed/expired), consistent with a faster platform-level takedown compared to independently-registered phishing domains.

## 5. Social Engineering Tactics
- [x] Authority impersonation (Brazilian tax authority / bank code "[BB]")
- [x] Financial incentive bait ("balance released")
- [x] Fake protocol/reference number for legitimacy
- [x] PDF attachment as delivery vector rather than a body link

## 6. Red Flags Summary
1. Sent from a personal Gmail address, not any official government or bank domain — despite full SPF/DKIM/DMARC pass
2. PDF attachment is effectively an image with a full-page invisible link, not a genuine document
3. Destination abuses a trusted cloud hosting platform (Azure) rather than a standalone domain
4. Fabricated tax-refund/balance-release narrative with a fake reference number

## 7. Verdict
**Classification:** Phishing (PDF/cloud-hosting delivery variant)
**Confidence:** High
**Analyst summary:** Demonstrates that authentication passing (SPF/DKIM/DMARC) does not equal sender legitimacy when a genuine free-mail account is used. The PDF attachment is a disguised full-page link rather than real document content, and the destination abuses Azure's trusted static-hosting domain to reduce the chance of blocklisting — infrastructure has since been taken down (DNS no longer resolves).
